window.CS_NARR=window.CS_NARR||{};window.CS_NARR["ml"]={"atlas": [[null, ["Hi, it's Buddy again. This sheet covers machine learning end to end: the maths, classical models, deep learning, large language models, and how all of it runs in production. That's a huge field, so we'll start with the map, and I'll keep coming back to one idea: choose the task and the evaluation before you choose the model."]], ["end-to-end-topic-map", ["The atlas splits the field into seven areas. Foundations: probability, statistics, linear algebra, optimisation, data quality and metrics. Classical machine learning: linear models, trees and boosting, clustering, dimensionality reduction, anomaly detection. Sequential and relational learning: time series, recommenders, causal inference, bandits and reinforcement learning, and graph learning. Deep learning: backpropagation, the main architectures, self supervised learning, multimodal models, and efficient models like mixtures of experts. Generative modelling: autoencoders, GANs, diffusion and LLM pretraining. LLM adaptation and applications: prompting, fine tuning, preference tuning, retrieval augmented generation, agents, safety and evaluation. And production and governance: inference, serving, MLOps, monitoring, privacy and fairness. Every chip jumps to the detailed card."]], ["route-by-the-learning-objective", ["This diagram starts from the decision you need to make, not from a model. If you need a class or a number, it's supervised learning. If you need an ordered list, it's ranking, usually retrieve then rank. Future values are forecasting, and you always start with a naive baseline. Groups or compact representations are unsupervised learning. Rare events are anomaly detection, where simple rules come before isolation forests or autoencoders. The effect of an intervention is causal inference: run an experiment if you can. Actions with delayed rewards point to bandits or reinforcement learning. And new content is generative modelling. Then the data modality refines the choice: for tabular data, a linear baseline and then gradient boosted trees; for images, audio and text, a pretrained representation that you fine tune. The footnote is the real lesson: check label availability, data volume, leakage risk, latency, interpretability and cost before you commit.", "The takeaway: Start from the decision needed: class or value → supervised, ordered results → ranking, groups → clustering, text → LLM.", "Pause and answer: The output is an ordered list of items. Which family?", "⏸", "Answer: Ranking or recommendation."]], ["classical-ml-lifecycle-and-decision-gates", ["Here's the lifecycle of a classical ML project. Define the problem and a success metric. Collect, inspect and label data. Split it the way the model will be used: by time if you predict the future, by user or site if you'll serve new users or sites. Build a simple baseline. Engineer features and try candidate models with cross validation. Check the holdout, calibration and performance on important slices. Then a gate: do quality, fairness, latency and cost all pass? If not, inspect the errors and go back. If yes, release carefully through a canary, shadow or A/B test, and monitor drift and quality, feeding back into data collection. The note on this card is one of the most important sentences in applied ML: split before you fit any learned transform. If you fit a scaler or an encoder on all the data, your test score is quietly inflated.", "The takeaway: Problem and metric, data and labels, a leakage-safe split, a simple baseline, features and models, validation, then deploy and monitor.", "Pause and answer: Why build a simple baseline early?", "⏸", "Answer: Every fancier model must beat it to justify its cost."]], ["choose-an-llm-application-architecture", ["For LLM applications, start with a strong existing model and ask what's missing. If nothing beyond instructions is missing, a good prompt with structured output is enough. If the model lacks fresh, private or citable knowledge, add retrieval augmented generation. If you need stable behaviour, style or format, fine tune, usually with LoRA. If the task needs actions in other systems, build a bounded tool workflow or agent. Only if the capability itself is missing do you distil, continue pretraining or train. Every option then goes through evaluation of quality, safety, latency and cost, and a red team gate, before a canary release with rollback ready. Remember the rule of thumb: RAG for changing facts and grounding, fine tuning for repeatable behaviour, and agents only when a tool action is genuinely needed.", "The takeaway: Start with a strong model and prompts; add RAG for fresh or private knowledge, tools for actions, fine-tuning for behaviour.", "Pause and answer: What's missing if you need fresh, private or cited knowledge?", "⏸", "Answer: Retrieval: add RAG."]], ["evaluation-is-a-stack-not-one-score", ["Evaluation is a stack. At the bottom, data and splits: is the test set representative and uncontaminated? Then task quality, with the right metric for the task. Then robustness and slices: where does it fail and for whom? For LLM and RAG systems: are answers grounded, useful and safe? Then production: latency, availability, cost. And finally online evidence from shadow, canary and A/B tests. The traps listed here are classic. Near duplicates across train and test inflate scores, so deduplicate before splitting. Tuning on the test set turns it into a training set. Averages hide failing slices. An offline gain isn't a business gain until an online test confirms it. LLM judges drift, so pin the judge's model and prompt. And always pick the metric for the decision it drives: precision at k for a feed, calibration for a threshold, recall for a safety filter.", "The takeaway: Evaluate data and split, task quality, robustness and fairness, cost and latency, and online impact.", "Pause and answer: First layer of the evaluation stack?", "⏸", "Answer: Data and split: representative and uncontaminated."]], ["coverage-map-detailed-reference-sections", ["The coverage map is a directory. Problem framing and model choice live in the selection section. Data and classical ML have their own section. Deep learning and generative models, LLM training and applications, inference and serving, and system design with MLOps each have theirs. Use it when you want to revise one family in depth.", "The takeaway: The coverage map points each topic family to its detailed section.", "Pause and answer: What does the coverage map help with?", "⏸", "Answer: Jumping to the section that covers a topic family."]], ["advanced-routes-to-recognize", ["These are the advanced routes. Personalised ranking at scale is retrieval, then a feature rich ranker, then re-ranking, evaluated offline with NDCG and recall and online with an experiment, while watching for position and popularity bias. Estimating the impact of a treatment means a randomised experiment if possible, and otherwise matching, weighting or doubly robust methods with clearly stated assumptions. Choosing actions from feedback starts with contextual bandits, and only moves to full reinforcement learning when delayed state really matters. Relational data suggests graph neural networks, but compare them with simpler baselines and guard against leakage through neighbours. Multimodal inputs use pretrained encoders per modality, then fusion. Small labelled datasets call for transfer learning, parameter efficient fine tuning and active labelling. And when quality regresses after launch, trace the path from data to features to model to serving to user outcome, to separate drift from retrieval failures and service failures.", "The takeaway: Personalised ranking, retrieval at scale and similar advanced routes each have a recognisable shape and key check.", "Pause and answer: Personalised ranking at scale: the route?", "⏸", "Answer: Candidate retrieval, a feature-rich ranker, then re-ranking with constraints."]]], "map": [[null, ["This short section answers the most common opening question in an ML interview: which algorithm would you start with?"]], ["algorithm-selection-start-here", ["For tabular data with mixed types, start with gradient boosting like XGBoost or LightGBM: it handles missing values and non linear interactions natively and usually wins on accuracy. If you need interpretability, logistic regression with good features gives meaningful, calibrated coefficients. For images, fine tune a pretrained CNN or vision transformer. For text classification, fine tune a pretrained language model. For anomalies without labels, isolation forest, DBSCAN or an autoencoder. For small data with odd structure, k nearest neighbours or an RBF kernel SVM. But always build a simple baseline first, because it tells you whether complexity buys anything. Two notes interviewers like. Random forest versus XGBoost: with noisy data or little time to tune, random forest; with time to tune, XGBoost, which is more sensitive to outliers because it fits residuals. And logistic regression is linear in the log odds, so if the true boundary is non linear, the model has bias, and more data won't fix that. Only better features or a more flexible model will.", "The takeaway: Tabular → gradient boosting; interpretable → logistic regression; images → CNNs or ViTs; text → transformers.", "Pause and answer: First choice for tabular mixed-type data?", "⏸", "Answer: XGBoost or LightGBM."]]], "math": [[null, ["Interviewers often ask you to derive or explain something on the spot, and it nearly always starts from a few pieces of maths. Let's make them feel intuitive."]], ["linear-algebra", ["The dot product measures alignment: the length of one vector times the length of the other times the cosine of the angle between them. That's why it appears as similarity, in projections, and in attention scores. Multiplying an m by n matrix with an n by p matrix costs m times n times p operations, and a linear layer is just y equals W x plus b. Eigenvectors are directions a matrix only stretches; the eigenvectors of a covariance matrix are the principal components. Singular value decomposition factors any matrix into rotations and scalings, and keeping only the largest singular values gives the best low rank approximation, which is the intuition behind PCA, matrix factorisation in recommenders, and LoRA. Rank is the number of independent directions, and low rank means compressible. Norms measure size: L1 encourages sparsity, L2 measures energy. And positive semi definite matrices, like covariance and kernel matrices, give convex quadratic forms.", "The takeaway: Dot products measure similarity, matrix multiplies are layers, eigenvectors of covariance are principal components.", "Pause and answer: Cost of multiplying (m×n) by (n×p)?", "⏸", "Answer: m·n·p."]], ["calculus-and-optimisation", ["The chain rule is backpropagation: the gradient of the loss with respect to an input equals the gradient with respect to the output times the local derivative. The gradient points uphill, so we step against it. The Jacobian generalises the derivative for vector outputs, and the Hessian describes curvature. Convex problems like linear regression, logistic regression and SVMs have no bad local minima. Neural networks aren't convex, but in high dimensions saddle points are a bigger nuisance than bad local minima. Lagrange multipliers handle constraints, which is how the SVM dual is derived. And the learning rate is the most important knob: too high diverges, too low crawls, so modern training uses a warmup followed by decay.", "The takeaway: The chain rule is backprop, gradients point uphill, and convex problems have no bad local minima.", "Pause and answer: What is backprop, mathematically?", "⏸", "Answer: Repeated application of the chain rule."]], ["probability-essentials", ["Bayes' rule says the probability of A given B equals the probability of B given A, times the prior of A, divided by the probability of B. Expectation is linear, so the expectation of a sum is the sum of expectations, always. Variance scales with the square: scaling a variable by a scales its variance by a squared. Variance is the expectation of the square minus the square of the expectation. Independence means probabilities multiply, and conditional independence is the assumption behind naive Bayes. The central limit theorem says sample means become approximately normal, with standard error sigma over the square root of n. And remember the base rate fallacy. A test that's ninety nine percent accurate for a disease affecting one percent of people produces roughly as many false positives as true positives, so a positive result is only about fifty percent likely to be real. That's why precision collapses when positives are rare.", "The takeaway: Bayes' rule, linearity of expectation, variance identities, independence and the CLT underpin ML.", "Pause and answer: Bayes' rule?", "⏸", "Answer: P(A|B) = P(B|A)·P(A) ÷ P(B)."]], ["distributions-to-know", ["These are the distributions to know and what each one models. Bernoulli: a single yes or no trial. Binomial: the number of successes in n trials. Poisson: counts of events in an interval, where the mean equals the variance. Geometric: the number of trials until the first success. Exponential: the time between events, which is memoryless. Normal: sums of many small effects, with the sixty eight, ninety five, ninety nine point seven rule for one, two and three standard deviations. Beta: a distribution over a probability, which is the prior in Thompson sampling. And categorical with its Dirichlet prior for multi class outcomes.", "The takeaway: Bernoulli, binomial, Poisson, geometric, normal and more, each with a mean and variance to know.", "Pause and answer: Poisson mean and variance?", "⏸", "Answer: Both λ."]], ["estimation-and-information-theory", ["Maximum likelihood estimation chooses parameters that make the data most probable. If you assume Gaussian noise, maximising likelihood is the same as minimising mean squared error; with Bernoulli outcomes, it's minimising cross entropy. That's why those losses are the defaults. Maximum a posteriori adds a prior: a Gaussian prior is L2 regularisation, a Laplace prior is L1. Entropy measures uncertainty; cross entropy measures the cost of coding data from p with a code built for q; and KL divergence is the gap between them. KL is asymmetric, and you'll meet it in VAEs, in the RLHF penalty and in distillation. The bias variance decomposition says expected error is bias squared plus variance plus irreducible noise: more capacity lowers bias and raises variance. And mutual information measures how much one variable tells you about another, which is useful for feature selection.", "The takeaway: MLE maximises likelihood; MAP adds a prior (Gaussian = L2, Laplace = L1); cross-entropy and KL measure distribution gaps.", "Pause and answer: Which prior corresponds to L2 regularisation?", "⏸", "Answer: A Gaussian prior."]], ["hypothesis-tests-and-a-b-testing", ["A p value is the probability of seeing data at least this extreme if the null hypothesis were true. It is not the probability that the null is true, and saying so in an interview is a red flag. Type one errors are false positives, controlled by alpha; type two are missed effects, and power is one minus beta, usually targeted at eighty percent. A handy rule: the sample size per arm is about sixteen times the variance divided by the square of the effect size you want to detect, at alpha of five percent and eighty percent power. Choose the test by the data: t tests for means, z or chi squared for proportions, Mann Whitney when data isn't normal, or bootstrap confidence intervals. Pitfalls include peeking at results early, which inflates false positives unless you use sequential tests; testing many metrics without correction; novelty effects; sample ratio mismatch, which signals a bug in assignment; and interference between users. CUPED reduces variance by subtracting a pre experiment covariate. And always watch guardrail metrics like latency and revenue alongside your primary metric.", "The takeaway: A p-value is P(data this extreme | H₀); aim for power 0.8; sample size per arm ≈ 16σ²/δ².", "Pause and answer: What does a p-value measure?", "⏸", "Answer: P(data this extreme | H₀), not P(H₀ true)."]], ["causal-inference-toolkit", ["Causal inference asks what would have happened without the treatment, which we can never observe directly. Randomised experiments solve that by design. Without randomisation, each method buys identification with an assumption. Regression adjustment assumes you measured every confounder. Propensity matching and weighting assume no unmeasured confounding and overlap between groups. Doubly robust methods are correct if either the outcome model or the propensity model is right. Difference in differences assumes parallel trends. Synthetic control builds a weighted mix of untreated units that mimics the treated one, useful when only one city or country was treated. Regression discontinuity compares units just above and below a threshold. Instrumental variables use something that affects the outcome only through the treatment. And uplift models target the users the treatment changes most. Learn the vocabulary: ATE is the average effect for everyone, ATT for the treated, CATE conditional on features. And draw the causal graph: adjust for confounders, but never for colliders, or for mediators of the effect you want to measure.", "The takeaway: Randomised experiments, regression adjustment, matching, diff-in-diff, IV and RDD each rest on a key assumption.", "Pause and answer: Difference-in-differences key assumption?", "⏸", "Answer: Parallel trends."]]], "formulas": [[null, ["This section collects the formulas you should be able to write from memory. Let me explain what each one is really measuring."]], ["classification-metrics", ["Precision asks: of everything I flagged, how much was right? Recall asks: of everything that was really positive, how much did I catch? F1 is their harmonic mean, which punishes imbalance between them, and F beta weights recall beta times as much. Specificity is recall for the negative class, and the false positive rate is one minus specificity. Log loss rewards confident correct probabilities and heavily punishes confident mistakes. ROC AUC is the probability that a random positive is scored above a random negative, so it measures ranking and is threshold free. With rare positives, prefer PR AUC, whose baseline is the positive rate rather than one half. Choose the threshold from business costs, not from point five. And know the averaging modes: macro averages per class equally, micro pools all counts, weighted averages by class size.", "The takeaway: Precision, recall, F1, specificity, ROC-AUC and PR-AUC each answer a different error-cost question.", "Pause and answer: Precision formula?", "⏸", "Answer: TP ÷ (TP + FP)."]], ["regression-ranking-and-generation-metrics", ["For regression, MAE is the average absolute error, robust to outliers; RMSE squares errors first, so it punishes large misses; R squared is the share of variance explained. For ranking, DCG sums the gain of relevant items discounted by the log of their position, and NDCG divides by the ideal ordering so scores lie between zero and one. MRR is the average of one over the rank of the first relevant result. Recall at k is the share of all relevant items that appear in the top k. For language models, perplexity is the exponential of the average cross entropy per token, roughly the effective number of choices the model hesitates between. MAPE explodes near zero, so use alternatives for such targets. And for generation, BLEU measures n gram precision, ROUGE recall for summaries, and BERTScore embedding similarity, all of which are weak proxies for real quality.", "The takeaway: MAE and RMSE for regression, NDCG, MRR and Recall@k for ranking, BLEU, ROUGE and judges for generation.", "Pause and answer: MRR formula?", "⏸", "Answer: Mean of 1 ÷ rank of the first relevant result."]], ["loss-functions", ["Loss functions encode what you care about. Mean squared error for regression, mean absolute error when outliers shouldn't dominate, and Huber as the compromise: quadratic near zero and linear further out. Cross entropy for classification and language modelling. Focal loss down weights easy examples, which helps with heavy imbalance, especially in object detection. Hinge loss is the SVM's margin loss. Contrastive and InfoNCE losses pull matching pairs together and push others apart, which trains embedding models like CLIP. Triplet loss requires the anchor to be closer to the positive than to the negative by a margin. KL divergence appears in VAEs, distillation and RLHF. And pairwise ranking loss, a logistic loss on score differences, trains rankers and reward models.", "The takeaway: MSE, MAE and Huber for regression, cross-entropy for classification, focal for imbalance, contrastive for embeddings.", "Pause and answer: Which regression loss resists outliers?", "⏸", "Answer: Huber (or MAE)."]], ["optimiser-updates", ["Plain SGD steps against the gradient. Momentum keeps a running velocity, which smooths noisy gradients and speeds up travel along consistent directions. Adam keeps two running averages, the mean of gradients and the mean of squared gradients, corrects their bias in early steps, and divides one by the square root of the other, so every parameter gets its own adaptive step size. AdamW applies weight decay directly to the weights instead of folding it into the gradient, which matters because Adam's adaptive scaling would otherwise distort the regularisation. And note the memory cost: Adam keeps two extra numbers per parameter, so mixed precision training with fp32 master weights needs about sixteen bytes per parameter before activations.", "The takeaway: SGD, momentum, Adam with bias correction, and AdamW with decoupled weight decay.", "Pause and answer: What does AdamW change from Adam?", "⏸", "Answer: Weight decay is decoupled from the gradient update."]], ["neural-net-building-blocks", ["The building blocks. The sigmoid squashes to between zero and one; softmax turns a vector of scores into a probability distribution. Attention computes softmax of Q times K transposed over the square root of the key dimension, times V. LayerNorm normalises each token's features to zero mean and unit variance and then rescales; RMSNorm skips the mean and only rescales by the root mean square, which is cheaper and works just as well in LLMs. SwiGLU is a gated feed forward layer used in most modern LLMs. And dropout zeroes units with probability p during training and scales the survivors by one over one minus p, so nothing needs to change at inference.", "The takeaway: Sigmoid, softmax, scaled dot-product attention, LayerNorm, RMSNorm and SwiGLU are the building blocks.", "Pause and answer: Attention formula?", "⏸", "Answer: softmax(QKᵀ ÷ √d_k) V."]], ["llm-sizing-maths", ["These sizing formulas let you do back of the envelope maths in an LLM interview. A dense decoder has roughly twelve times layers times the model width squared parameters, plus the embeddings. Training costs about six times parameters times tokens in floating point operations, and inference about two times parameters per generated token. Weight memory is parameters times bytes per parameter: two for bf16, one for int8, a half for int4. So a seven billion parameter model in bf16 needs about fourteen gigabytes. Full fine tuning with Adam needs sixteen to twenty bytes per parameter plus activations. The KV cache per token is two, for keys and values, times layers times KV heads times head dimension times bytes. For a seventy billion parameter Llama style model with grouped query attention in bf16, that's about a third of a megabyte per token, which is why long contexts and many concurrent users eat memory. And attention cost grows with the square of the sequence length.", "The takeaway: Params ≈ 12·L·d²; training FLOPs ≈ 6ND; inference ≈ 2N per token; weights memory = N × bytes per param.", "Pause and answer: Training FLOPs formula?", "⏸", "Answer: About 6 × parameters × tokens."]]], "cml": [[null, ["Now the classical ML toolkit: data preparation, the classic models, evaluation and the practical workflow in scikit-learn."]], ["data-validation", ["Data work comes first. Missing values can be missing completely at random, at random given other features, or not at random, where the missingness itself carries information; impute and add a missing indicator so the model can use that signal. Outliers might be errors or the most important examples, so investigate before removing them. Scale features for linear models and neural networks, and use log or Yeo Johnson transforms for skew. Encode categories with one hot, ordinal when order is real, target encoding with smoothing computed out of fold, or frequency encoding. Leakage is the silent killer: preprocessing before splitting, using the target, using future information, or letting the same group appear in train and test. Choose cross validation to match the data: stratified for imbalance, time series splits for temporal data, group splits when users repeat, and nested cross validation when you tune hyperparameters. And monitor drift with tools like the population stability index or a KS test.", "The takeaway: Handle missingness types, judge outliers, scale and encode correctly, and validate on leakage-safe splits.", "Pause and answer: Good practice when imputing?", "⏸", "Answer: Add a missing-indicator feature."]], ["classical-ml", ["This table summarises the classic algorithms. L1 regularisation produces sparse weights because its diamond shaped constraint has corners on the axes, while L2 shrinks smoothly. SVMs maximise the margin and use the kernel trick to work in high dimensional spaces implicitly. Trees split by Gini or entropy and have high variance on their own. Bagging and random forests reduce variance by averaging many trees, with a free out of bag estimate. Boosting reduces bias by fitting trees one after another on the remaining errors, and the learning rate trades off against the number of trees. KNN needs scaled features and suffers in high dimensions. Naive Bayes assumes independence and is surprisingly good for text. K-means needs k-means plus plus initialisation and assumes round clusters. DBSCAN finds arbitrary shapes and labels noise. Gaussian mixtures use the EM algorithm. PCA finds directions of maximum variance. t-SNE and UMAP are for visualisation. And stacking trains a meta model on out of fold predictions.", "The takeaway: L1 sparsifies, L2 shrinks; SVMs maximise margin; trees are high-variance, fixed by bagging or boosting.", "Pause and answer: Which regulariser produces sparse weights?", "⏸", "Answer: L1."]], ["evaluation", ["Which metric when? Accuracy only when classes are balanced and errors cost the same. Precision when false positives are costly, like flagging good email as spam. Recall when misses are costly, like fraud or cancer. F1 to balance them under imbalance. ROC AUC for ranking quality when classes are roughly balanced; PR AUC when they're heavily imbalanced. Log loss, Brier score and expected calibration error when probabilities themselves matter. MAE, RMSE and MAPE for regression. NDCG, MAP and MRR for ranking and retrieval. And for generation, automatic metrics are weak, so add human or LLM judge evaluation.", "The takeaway: Choose metrics by error costs: precision when false positives hurt, recall when misses hurt.", "Pause and answer: Fraud detection favours which metric?", "⏸", "Answer: Recall (false negatives are costly)."]], ["time-series-hpo", ["For time series, check stationarity, read the autocorrelation plots to choose ARIMA orders, and consider seasonal models, Holt Winters or Prophet. Validate walking forward through time, and give tree models lag and rolling features. Deep options include temporal convolutional networks and transformer based forecasters, and there are now pretrained foundation forecasters too. For hyperparameter optimisation, progress from grid search to random search with log uniform ranges, to Bayesian optimisation like Optuna's tree Parzen estimator, to early stopping schemes like Hyperband and ASHA, and population based training for large runs.", "The takeaway: Check stationarity, use walk-forward validation, lag features for trees, and search hyperparameters smartly.", "Pause and answer: Validation scheme for time series?", "⏸", "Answer: Walk-forward (rolling or expanding)."]], ["sklearn-workflow-your-template", ["This is your scikit-learn template, and the order matters. Split first, stratified. Build a column transformer with an imputer and scaler for numeric columns and a one hot encoder that ignores unknown categories. Put the preprocessing and the model in one pipeline, so cross validation fits the preprocessing inside each fold and nothing leaks. Cross validate, tune with randomized search using the step name, double underscore, parameter naming, fit on the training set, evaluate once on the test set, and save the whole pipeline so serving uses exactly the same preprocessing.", "The takeaway: Split first, then a ColumnTransformer inside a Pipeline, fit on train only, tune with CV.", "Pause and answer: Why split before any fitting?", "⏸", "Answer: To prevent leakage of test statistics."]], ["sklearn-mistakes-checklist", ["And the mistakes to avoid: fitting transforms on all the data before splitting; calling fit transform on the test set, which relearns statistics, when you should only transform; forgetting handle unknown in the one hot encoder; applying SMOTE outside the cross validation folds; trusting accuracy on imbalanced data; not fixing random states; and grid searching when randomized search would find as good a result in a fraction of the time.", "The takeaway: Never fit on full data, only transform the test set, ignore unknown categories, and oversample only inside CV folds.", "Pause and answer: Why not fit_transform on the test set?", "⏸", "Answer: It re-learns statistics from test data; use transform only."]]], "algos": [[null, ["This section goes deeper into how each classic algorithm learns, what to tune, and the follow up question you'll probably get."]], ["algorithm-reference", ["Let me walk through the reference table. Linear regression minimises squared error, with a closed form solution or gradient descent; it's fast and interpretable but distorted by outliers and correlated features. Logistic regression applies a sigmoid to a linear score and minimises log loss, which is convex; it gives calibrated probabilities but needs feature engineering for non linear boundaries. A decision tree makes greedy splits that most reduce impurity; it's readable but overfits without depth limits. A random forest averages deep trees trained on bootstrap samples with random feature subsets, which decorrelates them. Gradient boosting adds shallow trees that fit the negative gradient of the loss, and needs early stopping. SVMs maximise the margin and are strong on small high dimensional data but scale poorly. KNN has no training but slow prediction. Naive Bayes is fast and a great text baseline but poorly calibrated. K-means alternates assignment and centroid updates. DBSCAN grows clusters from dense core points. Gaussian mixtures give soft assignments with EM. PCA projects onto the top eigenvectors of the covariance. t-SNE and UMAP preserve local neighbourhoods for plots, but distances between clusters mean little. And isolation forest scores anomalies by how quickly random splits isolate them.", "The takeaway: Know how each algorithm learns, its key hyperparameters, strengths and pitfalls.", "Pause and answer: Linear regression closed form?", "⏸", "Answer: (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy."]], ["boosting-libraries-compared", ["The three boosting libraries. XGBoost uses second order information, both gradient and Hessian, with explicit regularisation and sparsity aware splits; it's the mature general default. LightGBM grows trees leaf wise and bins features into histograms, with gradient based sampling, so it's very fast on large data, but you must limit the number of leaves to avoid overfitting. CatBoost uses ordered boosting and ordered target statistics, which handle high cardinality categorical features without leakage and with little tuning. And remember the core contrast: bagging averages independent high variance models to reduce variance, while boosting chains weak learners to reduce bias.", "The takeaway: XGBoost is the mature default, LightGBM is fastest on big data, CatBoost handles categoricals best.", "Pause and answer: Which library excels with categorical features?", "⏸", "Answer: CatBoost."]], ["tree-split-maths", ["Gini impurity is one minus the sum of squared class proportions; entropy is the negative sum of p log p. Information gain is the parent's impurity minus the weighted impurity of the children. For regression trees, choose the split that most reduces variance. Gini and entropy usually choose similar splits, and Gini is cheaper because there's no logarithm. Information gain favours features with many distinct values, which gain ratio corrects. And impurity based feature importance is biased towards high cardinality features, so prefer permutation importance or SHAP.", "The takeaway: Gini and entropy measure impurity; gain is the impurity drop; regression trees reduce variance.", "Pause and answer: Gini impurity formula?", "⏸", "Answer: 1 − Σ pₖ²."]]], "feat": [[null, ["This section is about turning raw data into signal, and about showing that a model is understandable, fair and respectful of personal data."]], ["feature-engineering-playbook", ["The playbook goes data type by data type. Numeric features: standardise for linear models and networks, log transform skewed values, clip outliers, and bin when the effect is monotone but non linear. Low cardinality categories: one hot. High cardinality: target encoding computed out of fold with smoothing, frequency encoding, hashing, or learned embeddings. Dates and times: hour, weekday and holidays, cyclical sine and cosine encodings so midnight is next to eleven pm, time since the last event, and lags and rolling windows. Text: TF-IDF and n grams, or pretrained embeddings. Aggregates: per user and per item counts, rates and recency. Missing values: impute plus an indicator. Feature selection: filters, wrappers or embedded methods. And every row has a pitfall that's a form of leakage: fitting on all the data, encoding with the label in fold, or computing aggregates with information from after the prediction time. The phrase to remember is point in time correctness.", "The takeaway: Each data type has standard techniques and a classic leakage pitfall.", "Pause and answer: Pitfall when scaling numeric features?", "⏸", "Answer: Fitting the scaler on all data leaks test information."]], ["explainability", ["Explainability methods differ in scope. Coefficients are global but only comparable on scaled features. Permutation importance is model agnostic, but correlated features share and hide importance. SHAP gives additive attributions per prediction with game theoretic guarantees, and TreeSHAP is exact and fast for tree models. LIME fits a simple local surrogate but varies between runs. Partial dependence and ICE plots show how predictions change with one feature, but mislead when features are correlated. Counterfactuals answer \"what would need to change for approval\", which is great for users. In regulated domains, prefer interpretable models or monotonic constraints, and log the explanation shown with each decision.", "The takeaway: Coefficients, permutation importance, SHAP and partial dependence each have scope and caveats.", "Pause and answer: Weakness of permutation importance?", "⏸", "Answer: Correlated features share and hide importance."]], ["fairness", ["Fairness has several definitions that can conflict. Demographic parity asks for equal positive rates across groups. Equal opportunity asks for equal true positive rates. Equalised odds asks for equal true and false positive rates. Calibration within groups asks that a score means the same probability for everyone. When base rates differ between groups, calibration and equalised odds can't both hold, so the choice is a values decision you make with stakeholders, not a purely technical one. Bias enters through sampling, historical labels, measurement and proxies for protected attributes. Mitigation can happen before training by reweighting, during training with constraints, or after training with group specific thresholds. And the process matters: slice metrics by group, publish model cards, and keep humans in high impact decisions.", "The takeaway: Demographic parity, equal opportunity, equalised odds and calibration can't all hold at once.", "Pause and answer: Equal opportunity means?", "⏸", "Answer: Equal true-positive rates across groups."]], ["privacy-in-ml", ["Privacy techniques and risks. Differential privacy adds calibrated noise so that any one person barely changes the output; DP-SGD clips each example's gradient and adds noise, trading accuracy against the privacy budget epsilon. Federated learning trains on devices and only shares model updates, combined with secure aggregation, though updates can still leak without differential privacy. Handle personal data by minimising, pseudonymising, redacting from logs and prompts, and encrypting. Attacks include membership inference, testing whether someone was in the training set, which overfit models make easier, and training data extraction from LLMs, which deduplication and output filtering reduce. And the right to erasure is costly when data is baked into a model, so keep lineage.", "The takeaway: Differential privacy, federated learning and attack risks such as membership inference trade privacy against accuracy.", "Pause and answer: How does DP-SGD protect privacy?", "⏸", "Answer: Clips per-example gradients and adds noise."]]], "datac": [[null, ["In real projects, most gains come from better data rather than bigger models. This section is about labels, augmentation, anomalies and time series."]], ["labels-with-less-effort", ["Labels are expensive, so here are ways to get more from less effort. Start with clear labelling guidelines and measure agreement between annotators, for example with Cohen's kappa; if humans disagree, the model can't do better than them. Active learning labels the examples the model is least sure about. Weak supervision combines many noisy rules into probabilistic labels. Semi supervised learning pseudo labels confident unlabelled examples and enforces consistency under augmentation, as FixMatch does. Self supervised pretraining learns representations without labels, so you need only a few labelled examples to fine tune. LLMs can act as first pass labellers, as long as humans audit samples. And confident learning finds probably mislabelled examples so you can fix them.", "The takeaway: Guidelines with agreement checks, active learning, weak supervision and pseudo-labelling cut labelling cost.", "Pause and answer: What does active learning label?", "⏸", "Answer: The examples the model is most uncertain about."]], ["data-augmentation", ["Augmentation creates new training examples by transforming existing ones in ways that keep the label true. Images get flips, crops, colour jitter and mixing techniques like mixup and cutmix. Text gets back translation and paraphrasing. Audio gets SpecAugment masks, noise and speed changes. Tabular minority classes can use SMOTE, but only inside the cross validation folds. Time series get window slicing, jitter and time warping. The rule is simple: augment only training data, and only with transformations that preserve the label. Flipping a photo of a cat still gives a cat, but rotating a handwritten six by half a turn gives a nine, so that augmentation would corrupt the labels.", "The takeaway: Flips and mixup for images, back-translation for text, SpecAugment for audio.", "Pause and answer: Text augmentation via translation?", "⏸", "Answer: Back-translation."]], ["anomaly-detection", ["Anomaly detection usually starts simple. Statistical rules like z scores, interquartile ranges, or residuals after removing seasonality are a strong first line. Distance and density methods flag points far from their neighbours. Isolation forest builds random trees and notices that anomalies get isolated in very few splits. Autoencoders flag inputs they reconstruct badly. For metrics, forecast the expected value and alert when reality leaves the prediction interval. And because labels are rare, evaluate with precision at k and set the threshold from an alert budget, meaning how many alerts the analysts can actually investigate each day.", "The takeaway: Start statistical, then distance, density, isolation forest or autoencoders; labels are scarce, so evaluate carefully.", "Pause and answer: Why do isolation forests work?", "⏸", "Answer: Anomalies are isolated with fewer random splits."]], ["time-series-forecasting-in-depth", ["Forecasting in depth. Always start with baselines: the last value, or the value from the same time last season. Anything fancier must beat them, and surprisingly often it doesn't. Statistical models include ARIMA, exponential smoothing and Prophet. Machine learning approaches train gradient boosting on lags, rolling statistics and calendar features, often one model across many related series. Deep and foundation models include DeepAR for probabilistic forecasts and pretrained forecasters. Measure with scale free metrics like MASE or WAPE when comparing across series, and quantile loss for prediction intervals. Validate with rolling origin backtests and leave a gap equal to the forecast horizon, so you never accidentally use information you wouldn't have had. For hierarchies like store and region, reconcile forecasts so they add up.", "The takeaway: Beat naive baselines first; statistical models, tree models with lags, deep models and foundation models follow.", "Pause and answer: Baselines every forecast must beat?", "⏸", "Answer: Last value and seasonal naive."]]], "dl": [[null, ["Now deep learning: how training works, the main architectures, and generative models."]], ["deep-learning-core", ["The core ideas of training deep networks. Backpropagation multiplies local gradients, and since the sigmoid's derivative is at most a quarter, deep sigmoid networks suffer from vanishing gradients. Residual connections fix that by giving the gradient a direct path, because the derivative of x plus F of x includes a plus one. Exploding gradients are tamed by clipping the gradient norm, typically at one. Initialisation matters: He initialisation for ReLU, Xavier for tanh, and small scaled initialisations in transformers. Activations have moved from ReLU, which can die, to GELU, SiLU and SwiGLU. Optimisers are mostly AdamW with a warmup and cosine decay, with gradient accumulation to simulate larger batches. BatchNorm suits CNNs, while transformers use LayerNorm or RMSNorm in the pre norm position for stability. Mixed precision with bf16 needs no loss scaler, while fp16 does. And gradient checkpointing saves memory by recomputing activations during the backward pass.", "The takeaway: Backprop, residuals, gradient clipping and proper initialisation keep deep nets trainable.", "Pause and answer: Why do residual connections help?", "⏸", "Answer: They give a +1 gradient path through the identity."]], ["architectures", ["The architecture tour. CNNs exploit locality and share weights across positions; ResNets added skip connections. RNNs and LSTMs process sequences with gates, and the LSTM's cell state acts as a gradient highway. Attention computes weighted averages of values based on query key similarity, and we divide by the square root of the key dimension so the softmax doesn't saturate. The transformer stacks attention and feed forward layers with residuals and normalisation; encoders like BERT, decoders like GPT, and encoder decoders like T5. Positions can be sinusoidal, learned, rotary as in RoPE, or ALiBi biases. Multi head attention evolved into grouped query and multi query attention, which share keys and values across heads to shrink the KV cache. FlashAttention computes exact attention faster by tiling to fit in fast GPU memory. Mixture of experts routes each token to a few experts. State space models like Mamba run in linear time. Graph networks pass messages along edges. And scaling laws, like Chinchilla's roughly twenty training tokens per parameter, are heuristics for compute optimal training, not universal rules.", "The takeaway: CNNs exploit locality, RNN gates carry state, attention mixes all positions, transformers scale.", "Pause and answer: Why divide attention scores by √d_k?", "⏸", "Answer: To keep softmax from saturating."]], ["pytorch-loop-your-template", ["Your PyTorch training loop. Move the model to the device, then for each epoch: set training mode, and for each batch move it to the device, zero the gradients, compute the loss, call backward, clip the gradients, step the optimiser and the scheduler. Then switch to evaluation mode and run validation without gradients. The two modes matter because dropout and batch norm behave differently. For NaN losses, anomaly detection mode helps find the culprit, and the usual fixes are clipping, a lower learning rate, checking inputs and labels, and using bf16 instead of fp16. For transfer learning, freeze the backbone first, then unfreeze gradually with smaller learning rates for earlier layers.", "The takeaway: train(), zero_grad, forward, loss, backward, step; eval() with no_grad for validation.", "Pause and answer: Order of calls in a training step?", "⏸", "Answer: zero_grad, forward, loss, backward, step."]], ["generative-models", ["The generative model families. Autoencoders compress through a bottleneck. VAEs make the latent space probabilistic and train on the evidence lower bound, which is reconstruction minus a KL term, using the reparameterisation trick so gradients flow through the sampling step; their failure mode is posterior collapse. GANs pit a generator against a discriminator; they sample in one step but can suffer mode collapse. Diffusion models learn to predict the noise added to data, which is a stable regression problem, and generate by denoising step by step; DDIM takes fewer deterministic steps. Classifier free guidance is the dial between fidelity and diversity. Latent diffusion, as in Stable Diffusion, runs the process in a compressed latent space. Normalising flows give exact likelihoods. Flow matching regresses a velocity field and powers several recent image models. And consistency models distil sampling into one to four steps.", "The takeaway: Autoencoders, VAEs, GANs, flows, diffusion and autoregressive models trade quality, speed and stability.", "Pause and answer: VAE objective?", "⏸", "Answer: ELBO = reconstruction − KL."]], ["training-troubleshooting", ["This troubleshooting table is gold in interviews, because it shows you've actually trained models. If the loss doesn't decrease, check the learning rate, look for label or loss bugs, and try to overfit a single small batch first; if you can't, there's a bug. If the loss becomes NaN, suspect exploding gradients, log of zero, fp16 overflow or bad inputs. If training is good but validation is bad, it's overfitting, or leakage only in training, or a distribution shift. If both are bad, it's underfitting. If validation is better than training, that's usually dropout and augmentation being active only in training, which is fine. Sudden loss spikes call for warmup, a lower peak learning rate and checking bad batches. Out of memory calls for smaller micro batches with accumulation, checkpointing and mixed precision. An underused GPU usually means data loading is the bottleneck. And great offline but poor online usually means training serving skew.", "The takeaway: Overfit one batch first; check LR, labels, normalisation and leakage when training misbehaves.", "Pause and answer: First debugging step when loss won't decrease?", "⏸", "Answer: Overfit one small batch."]]], "domains": [[null, ["This section covers the applied domains that come up in ML engineer loops."]], ["recommender-systems", ["Recommenders are built as a funnel. Candidate generation narrows millions of items to thousands cheaply; ranking scores hundreds with a richer model; re-ranking applies diversity and business rules. Collaborative filtering uses the user item matrix, through item similarity or matrix factorisation. Two tower models encode users and items separately so retrieval becomes a nearest neighbour search, trained with in batch negatives. Rankers range from gradient boosted trees to deep models like DLRM and DIN, often multi task, predicting clicks and watch time together. Cold start uses content features, popularity priors and exploration. Feedback loops create position and popularity bias, so log propensities and keep exploration traffic. Measure offline with recall at k, NDCG and calibration, and online with click through, dwell time, retention and diversity.", "The takeaway: Candidate generation, ranking, re-ranking; collaborative filtering, two-towers and sequence models.", "Pause and answer: Three stages of the recommendation funnel?", "⏸", "Answer: Candidate generation, ranking, re-ranking."]], ["search-and-learning-to-rank", ["Search combines lexical and semantic retrieval. BM25 scores term matches with saturation and length normalisation. Dense retrieval uses bi encoders and an approximate nearest neighbour index. Hybrid search merges both with reciprocal rank fusion. Cross encoders or LLMs then re-rank the top results. Learning to rank can be pointwise, pairwise like RankNet, or listwise like LambdaMART. Labels come from human graded relevance or clicks corrected for position bias. And query understanding adds spelling correction, expansion and intent classification.", "The takeaway: BM25 plus dense retrieval, fused with RRF, then cross-encoder re-ranking and LTR.", "Pause and answer: BM25's two key parameters?", "⏸", "Answer: k₁ (term saturation) and b (length normalisation)."]], ["nlp", ["NLP has moved from bag of words and TF-IDF, through word2vec, to contextual models like BERT, and now LLM embeddings. Text classification still starts with a TF-IDF plus logistic regression baseline before fine tuning an encoder or prompting an LLM. Named entity recognition is token classification with BIO tags. Summarisation and translation use encoder decoders or decoder LLMs. Question answering can be extractive, picking a span, or generative with retrieval. And tokenisers like BPE, WordPiece and SentencePiece split text into subwords, with byte level vocabularies avoiding unknown tokens.", "The takeaway: From bag of words to contextual embeddings and LLMs; TF-IDF plus LR remains a strong baseline.", "Pause and answer: Strong text classification baseline?", "⏸", "Answer: TF-IDF + logistic regression."]], ["computer-vision", ["Computer vision tasks and their models. Classification with ResNet, EfficientNet, ConvNeXt or vision transformers, measured by top one and top five accuracy. Detection with two stage models like Faster R-CNN or one stage models like YOLO, measured by mean average precision at an IoU threshold, with non maximum suppression to remove duplicate boxes. Segmentation with U-Net, Mask R-CNN or the promptable SAM, measured by IoU and Dice. Vision language models connect an image encoder to an LLM through a projector, and CLIP learns a shared image text space contrastively. Image generation uses latent diffusion. In practice, augmentation and transfer learning do most of the work.", "The takeaway: Classification, detection, segmentation and their models and metrics: top-k accuracy, mAP and IoU.", "Pause and answer: One-stage versus two-stage detectors?", "⏸", "Answer: YOLO/SSD (one-stage) versus Faster R-CNN (two-stage)."]], ["speech-and-multimodal", ["Speech recognition uses CTC, RNN transducers or encoder decoders like Whisper, measured by word error rate. Text to speech goes from text to a spectrogram to a vocoder, or uses neural codec models, measured by mean opinion score. Voice agents chain streaming recognition, an LLM and streaming speech, within a latency budget of under a second, and must handle the user interrupting. And multimodal fusion can be early, late or through cross attention.", "The takeaway: ASR with CTC or encoder-decoder, TTS via spectrogram and vocoder, voice agents chaining or natively multimodal.", "Pause and answer: ASR metric?", "⏸", "Answer: Word error rate (WER)."]], ["reinforcement-learning-and-bandits", ["Reinforcement learning models the world as a Markov decision process: states, actions, transitions, rewards and a discount. The Bellman equation says a state's value is the best immediate reward plus the discounted value of where you land. Q-learning and DQN learn action values off policy, stabilised by a replay buffer and a target network. Policy gradients like REINFORCE push up the probability of actions that led to high returns, and a baseline reduces their high variance. Actor critic methods and PPO use advantage estimates, and PPO clips the policy ratio so updates stay small, which is also why it appears in RLHF. Bandits are the one step version: epsilon greedy, UCB, and Thompson sampling, with contextual bandits used widely for ads and recommendations. And offline RL learns from logged data, evaluated with importance sampling or doubly robust estimators.", "The takeaway: MDPs, Bellman equations, Q-learning, policy gradients and bandits trade exploration and exploitation.", "Pause and answer: What do DQN's replay buffer and target network do?", "⏸", "Answer: Stabilise off-policy TD learning."]], ["recommendation-funnel", ["The funnel diagram makes the numbers concrete: millions of items, about a thousand candidates, about a hundred ranked, about twenty shown. Clicks and watch time flow back into logs that train both the retrieval and ranking models, which is exactly where feedback loops come from.", "The takeaway: Millions of items narrow to about 1,000 candidates, about 100 ranked, then a re-ranked slate.", "Pause and answer: What does candidate generation use?", "⏸", "Answer: Two-tower ANN, co-visitation or popularity."]]], "tfx": [[null, ["Now let's open up a large language model and see what's inside."]], ["decoder-block-end-to-end", ["Here's the path of a token through a modern decoder. Text is tokenised into IDs, and each ID looks up an embedding vector. In each layer, the vector is normalised with RMSNorm, then goes through masked self attention, where rotary position embeddings are applied to queries and keys. The output is added back to the input, the residual connection. Then another normalisation, a feed forward network, usually SwiGLU with a hidden size two to four times the model width, or a mixture of experts layer, and another residual add. Repeat for all layers. A final normalisation feeds the language model head, which produces logits over the vocabulary, and we sample the next token. The causal mask lets each token attend only to itself and earlier tokens. Training processes all positions in parallel, which is teacher forcing, while generation produces one token at a time.", "The takeaway: Tokenise, embed, pre-norm, masked attention with RoPE, residual, pre-norm, SwiGLU FFN, residual; repeat; final norm and LM head.", "Pause and answer: Where is RoPE applied?", "⏸", "Answer: To Q and K inside attention."]], ["architecture-choices", ["The architecture choices. Attention heads evolved from full multi head, to grouped query attention sharing keys and values within groups, to multi query with a single key value head, and multi head latent attention which compresses the cache further. Positions evolved from absolute to rotary, which encodes relative position by rotating queries and keys. Long context relies on RoPE scaling methods, sliding window attention and sequence parallelism. Normalisation moved from post norm to pre norm for stable deep training, and to RMSNorm for speed. Feed forward layers moved to SwiGLU for better quality per FLOP. Mixture of experts adds parameters without adding compute per token, but needs a load balancing loss and expert parallelism. And encoders remain the choice for embeddings and classification, decoders for generation.", "The takeaway: GQA or MQA shrink the KV cache, RoPE gives relative positions, MoE scales parameters without scaling compute.", "Pause and answer: What does GQA do?", "⏸", "Answer: Shares K/V heads across groups of query heads to shrink the KV cache."]], ["decoding-strategies", ["Decoding turns probabilities into text. Greedy decoding always takes the most likely token, which is deterministic but can loop. Beam search keeps several candidate sequences, good for translation but bland for chat. Temperature divides the logits: below one sharpens the distribution, above one flattens it. Top k samples from the k most likely tokens; top p, or nucleus sampling, from the smallest set whose probability reaches p; min p keeps tokens above a fraction of the top probability, adapting to the model's confidence. Repetition penalties discourage reuse. Constrained decoding masks the logits with a grammar or JSON schema, guaranteeing valid output. And speculative decoding lets a small draft model propose tokens that the big model verifies in one pass, which speeds things up without changing the output distribution.", "The takeaway: Greedy and beam are deterministic; temperature, top-k and top-p sample with controlled randomness.", "Pause and answer: Temperature below 1 does what?", "⏸", "Answer: Sharpens the distribution: more deterministic."]], ["tokenisation-details", ["Tokenisation details. BPE starts from bytes or characters and repeatedly merges the most frequent pair. WordPiece merges by likelihood gain, and Unigram prunes a large vocabulary. A bigger vocabulary means shorter sequences but a bigger embedding table; thirty two thousand to two hundred fifty six thousand is typical. Tokenisation explains some odd failures: numbers split into awkward pieces hurt arithmetic, non English text often costs more tokens, and rare glitch tokens behave strangely. Special tokens mark the beginning and end of text, chat roles, and tool calls.", "The takeaway: BPE merges frequent pairs, WordPiece merges by likelihood, Unigram prunes a large vocab.", "Pause and answer: How does BPE build its vocabulary?", "⏸", "Answer: By repeatedly merging the most frequent pair."]], ["reasoning-models-and-test-time-compute", ["Reasoning models spend more compute at inference time. Chain of thought generates intermediate steps before the answer, so harder problems get more tokens. Self consistency samples many solutions and takes a majority vote, or a verifier picks the best. Process reward models score each step, while outcome reward models score only the final answer. Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards uses unit tests or checkable maths answers as the reward, which is how long reasoning is trained. GRPO is a PPO variant without a value network: it compares each sample's reward with the others for the same prompt. Search methods explore reasoning steps with a verifier. Thinking budgets cap reasoning tokens to trade accuracy against latency and cost. And reasoning can be distilled into small models by fine tuning them on long traces from a strong model.", "The takeaway: Chain of thought, self-consistency, search and RL-trained reasoning spend more compute at inference for better answers.", "Pause and answer: Self-consistency?", "⏸", "Answer: Sample many solutions and take the majority answer."]], ["decoder-block-diagram", ["This diagram is the same decoder block drawn as boxes. Notice the two circles with plus signs: those are the residual additions, and the arrows that skip around attention and the feed forward layer are the residual paths that keep gradients healthy in very deep stacks.", "The takeaway: Embedding, RMSNorm, masked attention with RoPE, GQA and KV cache, residual, RMSNorm, SwiGLU, residual.", "Pause and answer: What does the KV cache store?", "⏸", "Answer: Keys and values of past tokens for fast decoding."]]], "llm": [[null, ["This section covers the LLM lifecycle, alignment, prompting, agents and retrieval augmented generation."]], ["llm-lifecycle", ["The lifecycle of an LLM product. Pretrain on trillions of tokens with next token prediction. Supervised fine tuning on instruction and response pairs teaches the model to follow instructions. Alignment with RLHF or DPO teaches it which answers people prefer. Then evaluation and red teaming, quantisation or distillation for efficiency, serving on engines like vLLM, and monitoring with feedback that flows back into new fine tuning data.", "The takeaway: Pretrain, SFT, align, evaluate and red-team, quantise or distil, then serve.", "Pause and answer: What follows SFT?", "⏸", "Answer: Alignment: RLHF or DPO."]], ["training-alignment", ["Training and alignment methods. Pretraining needs curated, deduplicated data and checks for test set contamination. Supervised fine tuning depends far more on the quality and coverage of examples than on their count. LoRA trains small low rank adapters, and QLoRA does it on top of a four bit base model. RLHF trains a reward model on human preferences and optimises the policy with PPO, powerful but costly. DPO optimises directly on preference pairs without a reward model, cheaper and more stable. RLAIF and constitutional AI use AI feedback guided by principles. Distillation trains a small student on a big teacher's outputs. And synthetic data, like self instruct or execution verified code, needs auditing for quality and distribution shift.", "The takeaway: Pretraining for knowledge, SFT for behaviour, preference optimisation for alignment.", "Pause and answer: DPO versus RLHF?", "⏸", "Answer: DPO optimises preferences directly without a reward model or RL loop."]], ["prompting-agents", ["Prompting and agent techniques. Chain of thought asks the model to reason step by step, and self consistency votes over several samples. ReAct alternates thought, action and observation. Reflection lets the model critique and retry. Tree of thought searches over reasoning branches. Tool use relies on JSON schemas, argument validation and timeouts. Memory can be in context, short term, long term semantic, or structured state. Multi agent systems use orchestrators and workers, supervisors, or debate. The failure modes are hallucinated tool calls, wrong arguments, cascading errors and infinite loops, so budgets and human approval are essential. And DSPy optimises prompts programmatically against a metric.", "The takeaway: CoT, self-consistency, ReAct, reflection and tree of thought structure reasoning and tool use.", "Pause and answer: ReAct loop?", "⏸", "Answer: Thought → Action → Observation."]], ["rag", ["Retrieval augmented generation, stage by stage. Chunk documents into roughly two hundred to five hundred tokens with some overlap, ideally along the document's structure. Embed them; a fifteen hundred dimensional float vector is about six kilobytes, so a million vectors is about six gigabytes. Retrieve with a hybrid of dense vectors and BM25, merged with reciprocal rank fusion. Index with HNSW for high recall at the cost of memory, or IVF with product quantisation to compress. Improve queries with multi query rewriting, HyDE, which embeds a hypothetical answer, or step back questions. Re-rank the top results with a cross encoder for precision. Mind the lost in the middle effect by placing the best chunks at the edges of the context. Advanced variants include self RAG, corrective RAG and GraphRAG. And evaluate with faithfulness, answer relevance, and context precision and recall.", "The takeaway: Chunking, embeddings, hybrid retrieval with RRF, re-ranking, context building and grounded generation.", "Pause and answer: Typical chunk size and overlap?", "⏸", "Answer: 200–500 tokens with 15–25% overlap."]], ["rag-pipeline", ["The pipeline diagram shows the order: query, rewrite, hybrid retrieval, fusion, cross encoder re-ranking, context building, the LLM, and finally guardrails with citations. When an answer is wrong, walk this pipeline backwards to find which stage failed.", "The takeaway: Query rewrite, hybrid retrieval, RRF fusion, cross-encoder re-rank, context, LLM, guardrails and citations.", "Pause and answer: What is HyDE?", "⏸", "Answer: Generating a hypothetical answer to embed for retrieval."]], ["architecture-decision", ["This decision tree is a simpler cousin of the one in the atlas. If you need private data, use RAG. If not, and you need a strict format, fine tune with LoRA when you have a thousand or more good examples, otherwise use few shot prompting. Then, if the task needs tools or APIs, wrap it in an agentic workflow; if not, serve it directly.", "The takeaway: Private data → RAG; strict format with 1000+ examples → fine-tune; otherwise few-shot prompting.", "Pause and answer: Needs private data: first choice?", "⏸", "Answer: RAG."]], ["hallucination-stack", ["Hallucination deserves a full stack of defences. Causes include training with teacher forcing, rare facts, and sycophancy learned from RLHF. Detection uses self consistency, SelfCheckGPT, natural language inference checks and claim level fact scoring. Mitigation includes grounding with retrieval, permission to say \"I don't know\", constrained decoding and calibration. LLM judges have their own biases toward position, length and their own outputs. The guarded answer pipeline at the bottom is a pattern worth memorising: retrieve, and stop early if nothing relevant scores above a threshold; generate with an instruction to cite a passage for every claim; verify each claim against its passage and drop or flag unsupported ones; then answer with citations, or abstain and hand off to a human.", "The takeaway: Hallucinations come from training and incentives; detect with consistency and NLI, mitigate with grounding and abstention.", "Pause and answer: Name a detection method.", "⏸", "Answer: Any of: self-consistency, SelfCheckGPT, NLI checks, FActScore."]], ["embeddings-and-vector-search-internals", ["Vector search internals. A flat index compares against every vector, perfect recall and fine up to about a hundred thousand vectors. IVF clusters vectors and searches only the nearest clusters, controlled by how many clusters you probe. Product quantisation compresses vectors ten to fifty times at some recall cost, so you re-rank with full vectors. HNSW navigates a layered proximity graph and gives the best recall for a given latency, but uses lots of memory. DiskANN and ScaNN scale to billions. Practical notes: normalise vectors so cosine similarity equals the dot product; filter by tenant and permissions inside the index, because filtering afterwards can leave too few results; measure recall at k against exact search; and remember that changing the embedding model means re-embedding everything, so version your indexes.", "The takeaway: Flat search is exact, IVF clusters, HNSW graphs give fast approximate search, PQ compresses.", "Pause and answer: Up to roughly what size is flat search fine?", "⏸", "Answer: About 100K vectors."]]], "ft": [[null, ["This section is about training at scale, adapting models cheaply, and the preference optimisation family."]], ["distributed-training", ["How training spreads across GPUs. Data parallelism copies the model to every GPU and averages gradients. ZeRO and FSDP shard optimiser states, then gradients, then the parameters themselves, so memory per GPU falls as you add GPUs, at the cost of more communication. Tensor parallelism splits individual matrices across GPUs and needs fast links within a node. Pipeline parallelism splits layers across GPUs and uses micro batches to fill the idle bubbles. Sequence parallelism splits long sequences. Expert parallelism places mixture of experts across devices with all to all routing. Activation checkpointing recomputes activations to save memory for about thirty percent extra compute. And mixed precision computes in bf16 with fp32 master weights, or fp8 on the newest hardware.", "The takeaway: Data parallel copies the model, ZeRO/FSDP shard optimiser state, gradients and parameters, tensor and pipeline parallel split the model.", "Pause and answer: What does ZeRO-3 / FSDP shard?", "⏸", "Answer: Optimiser states, gradients and parameters."]], ["parameter-efficient-fine-tuning", ["Parameter efficient fine tuning. LoRA freezes the weight matrix and learns a low rank update, the product of two thin matrices, scaled by alpha over r. With ranks of eight to sixty four you train well under one percent of the parameters, and you can merge the update back so inference has no overhead. QLoRA keeps the frozen base in four bit NF4 with double quantisation and paged optimisers, which lets you fine tune a seventy billion parameter model on a single large GPU. DoRA separates magnitude and direction and applies LoRA to the direction, getting closer to full fine tuning. Adapters insert small bottleneck layers. Prefix and prompt tuning learn virtual tokens. And full fine tuning has the highest ceiling but the most memory and the greatest risk of forgetting. For quality, apply LoRA to all linear layers, not just attention.", "The takeaway: LoRA trains low-rank adapters (about 0.1–1% of params); QLoRA does it on a 4-bit frozen base.", "Pause and answer: LoRA weight update?", "⏸", "Answer: W' = W + (α/r)·BA with low-rank B and A."]], ["preference-optimisation-family", ["The preference optimisation family. Classic RLHF with PPO needs a reward model, a value model and a reference model, and maximises reward minus a KL penalty that keeps the policy close to the reference. DPO needs only chosen and rejected pairs and, in closed form, increases the log ratio of chosen over rejected relative to the reference. IPO, SimPO and ORPO are regularised or reference free variants, and ORPO merges supervised fine tuning with preference learning. KTO works with simple thumbs up or down labels. GRPO and RL with verifiable rewards use group relative advantages for reasoning and code. And RLAIF uses an AI judge guided by principles.", "The takeaway: RLHF uses a reward model and PPO with a KL penalty; DPO and relatives optimise preference pairs directly.", "Pause and answer: What does RLHF need beyond preference data?", "⏸", "Answer: A reward model, a value model and a reference policy."]], ["fine-tuning-decision-guide", ["The fine tuning decision guide in one breath: prompt first, then RAG for knowledge, then fine tuning for behaviour, style, format, latency or cost. Fine tuning does not reliably teach new facts; it changes how the model responds. A few thousand clean examples often beat a large noisy set. Hold out an evaluation set before you train and watch for regressions in general skills. Distillation, using a large model to label data for a small one, is a common way to cut serving cost. And track training loss, evaluation loss, the task metric and a safety regression suite.", "The takeaway: Prompting first, RAG for knowledge, fine-tuning for behaviour, style, format, latency or cost.", "Pause and answer: Does fine-tuning reliably add new facts?", "⏸", "Answer: No; it changes how the model responds."]], ["how-a-chat-model-is-built", ["This diagram shows how a chat model is built. Pretraining on web, code and books produces a base model. Supervised fine tuning teaches instruction following. Preference tuning with RLHF or DPO shapes which answers it prefers. Reinforcement learning on verifiable tasks strengthens reasoning. Then evaluations and red teaming before release.", "The takeaway: Pretraining makes a base model, SFT teaches instructions, preference tuning aligns it, then safety and evaluation.", "Pause and answer: What does pretraining optimise?", "⏸", "Answer: Next-token prediction on huge text corpora."]], ["prompt-rag-or-fine-tune", ["And this ladder tells you what to do when the output isn't good enough. Can a better prompt or examples fix it? Then prompt engineering. Is knowledge missing or changing? Then RAG with citations. Is the format, tone or behaviour wrong? Then supervised fine tuning or LoRA. Do you need preferred answers, not just acceptable ones? Then DPO or other preference tuning. Is it too slow or expensive? Then distil or quantise. And if none of these apply, improve your evaluations first, because you may not be measuring the real problem.", "The takeaway: Prompt problem → better prompts; missing knowledge → RAG; wrong behaviour or format → fine-tune; latency or cost → distil.", "Pause and answer: Missing or changing knowledge?", "⏸", "Answer: RAG with citations."]]], "agents": [[null, ["This section is about building reliable LLM applications: when to use workflows versus agents, how tools and memory work, and how to manage the context window."]], ["workflows-vs-agents", ["There's a spectrum from fixed workflows to autonomous agents, and the rule is to use the simplest thing that works. Prompt chaining runs a fixed sequence of calls with checks in between. Routing classifies the input and sends it to a specialised prompt or a cheaper model. Parallelisation splits a task into independent parts, or runs the same task several times and votes. Orchestrator and workers lets an LLM plan subtasks dynamically, which suits work like multi file code edits. Evaluator and optimiser loops generate, critique and revise. And a fully autonomous agent chooses tools in a loop until it's done, which needs sandboxing, budgets and checkpoints. Add autonomy only when it measurably helps, because every step of freedom is also a step where things can go wrong.", "The takeaway: Chaining, routing, parallelisation and orchestrator-worker are workflows; agents choose their own steps.", "Pause and answer: When is routing appropriate?", "⏸", "Answer: When inputs fall into distinct categories handled by specialised prompts or models."]], ["agent-anatomy", ["An agent has six parts. The model does the reasoning and chooses tools. Tools are functions with JSON schema arguments, clear names and descriptions, and they should return concise, structured results. Memory comes in four kinds: working memory is the context window, episodic memory is past runs, semantic memory is a vector store, and procedural memory is instructions and skills. Planning can be ReAct, plan and execute, self critique, or tree search. The environment is where actions happen: a code sandbox, a browser, files or APIs. And control is what keeps it safe: step and token budgets, timeouts, human approval for irreversible actions, and checkpoints so a long task can resume.", "The takeaway: A model, well-described tools, memory, planning and guardrails make an agent.", "Pause and answer: What makes a tool easy for a model to use?", "⏸", "Answer: A clear name and description, JSON-schema args and concise structured results."]], ["model-context-protocol-mcp", ["The Model Context Protocol standardises how AI applications connect to tools and data. The host is the AI app the user runs. It keeps one client connection per server. A server exposes capabilities from some system, like GitHub or a database. Servers offer tools, which the model invokes; resources, which are readable context; and prompts, which are reusable templates. Transport is standard input and output locally, or streamable HTTP with OAuth remotely. Why it matters: one integration works across many hosts, so the number of connectors grows as N plus M instead of N times M.", "The takeaway: MCP connects hosts to servers exposing tools, resources and prompts through a standard client protocol.", "Pause and answer: What's an MCP server?", "⏸", "Answer: A process exposing a system's capabilities (tools, resources, prompts)."]], ["context-engineering", ["Context engineering treats the context window as a budget shared by instructions, tool definitions, retrieved documents, history and scratch work. Put stable content first so prefix caching can reuse it, and volatile content last. Retrieve information just in time instead of preloading everything; return identifiers and let the agent fetch details. Compact long histories by summarising old turns and dropping stale tool outputs. Use sub agents with clean contexts for deep subtasks, returning condensed results. And for long running tasks, keep structured notes or memory files.", "The takeaway: Treat context as a budget: stable content first for caching, retrieve just enough, compact history.", "Pause and answer: Why put stable content first?", "⏸", "Answer: Prefix caching reuses it across calls."]], ["prompting-checklist", ["A prompting checklist. State the role and goal, and who the output is for. Give context and constraints, and describe what good looks like. Provide a few varied, representative examples. Specify the output format, with a schema, tags or JSON mode. Let the model reason before answering hard tasks. Tell it what to do when unsure. And version your prompts and test them against an evaluation set, exactly like code.", "The takeaway: Role and goal, context and constraints, varied examples, an explicit output format, and tests.", "Pause and answer: How should few-shot examples be chosen?", "⏸", "Answer: Varied and representative."]], ["frameworks-and-tooling-know-one-well", ["You don't need every framework, but know one well and know what the others are for. LangGraph for stateful graph workflows with checkpoints and human in the loop. LlamaIndex for data connectors and RAG pipelines. DSPy for prompts optimised against a metric. Provider agent SDKs for tool loops with little glue code. CrewAI and AutoGen for multi agent patterns. Pydantic and Instructor for typed structured outputs with validation and retries. Langfuse, LangSmith and Phoenix for tracing. And vLLM, SGLang and TensorRT-LLM for serving, with Ollama and llama.cpp for local models.", "The takeaway: LangGraph for stateful graphs, LlamaIndex for data and RAG, DSPy for optimised prompts; know one well.", "Pause and answer: Which framework optimises prompts against a metric?", "⏸", "Answer: DSPy."]], ["agent-loop", ["The agent loop diagram: the user's goal goes to the model, which chooses the next step. A tool call goes to a tool, API or sandbox, and the observation comes back. Risky actions go to a human for approval. Memory is read and written along the way. And the loop ends when the task is done or a budget is hit, with an answer that cites its sources. The note says guardrails sit on every arrow: validate tool arguments, cap steps and tokens, and treat tool output as data, never as instructions.", "The takeaway: The LLM picks a step, calls a tool, reads the observation, asks a human for risky actions, and repeats.", "Pause and answer: What should happen before a risky action?", "⏸", "Answer: Human approval."]]], "evalsafe": [[null, ["How do you prove an LLM system works, and how does it get attacked? That's this section."]], ["evaluation-methods", ["Evaluation methods for LLM systems. A golden test set is your regression gate on every prompt or model change; keep it fresh and keep it out of your prompts. Reference based metrics like exact match, F1 and unit tests work for checkable tasks but miss valid paraphrases. LLM as judge scales qualitative grading with a rubric, but has position, verbosity and self preference biases, so calibrate it against humans. Pairwise comparisons build Elo style rankings. RAG systems need retrieval and generation evaluated separately. Agents need task success, tool call accuracy, step counts, cost and trajectory review, and because they're non deterministic, run several trials. And online signals like thumbs, edits and escalations are useful but sparse and biased.", "The takeaway: Golden sets, reference metrics, LLM judges, human review and online signals each catch different failures.", "Pause and answer: What's a golden test set used for?", "⏸", "Answer: A regression gate on every prompt or model change."]], ["benchmarks-to-recognise", ["These are benchmark names you should recognise: MMLU and GPQA for knowledge and reasoning, GSM8K, MATH and AIME for maths, HumanEval and SWE-bench for code, tau bench, GAIA and WebArena for agents, IFEval and Chatbot Arena for instruction following and chat, needle in a haystack and RULER for long context, and TruthfulQA and HarmBench for truthfulness and safety. But the caveat matters most: public benchmarks saturate and leak into training data, so your own task specific evaluation matters more than any leaderboard.", "The takeaway: MMLU and GPQA for knowledge, GSM8K and MATH for maths, HumanEval and SWE-bench for code.", "Pause and answer: Name a coding benchmark.", "⏸", "Answer: Any of: HumanEval, MBPP, LiveCodeBench, SWE-bench."]], ["owasp-top-10-for-llm-applications-2025", ["The OWASP top ten for LLM applications is the security checklist. Number one is prompt injection, both direct, from the user, and indirect, hidden in documents, web pages or tool results. Then sensitive information disclosure, supply chain risks in models and plugins, data and model poisoning, and improper output handling, where unsanitised model output reaches SQL, a shell or HTML. Excessive agency means too many tools or permissions. System prompt leakage, weaknesses in vector stores, misinformation, and unbounded consumption, which covers runaway cost and denial of service, complete the list.", "The takeaway: Prompt injection tops the list, followed by sensitive data disclosure, supply chain and more.", "Pause and answer: LLM01?", "⏸", "Answer: Prompt injection (direct and indirect)."]], ["defence-in-depth", ["Defence in depth means no single control has to be perfect. Treat all retrieved and tool content as untrusted data. Give tools the least privilege, read only by default, with human approval for irreversible actions. Classify inputs and outputs for jailbreaks, personal data and toxicity, and allow list URLs and actions. Validate and escape model output before it reaches any interpreter. Sandbox code execution and control network egress. Limit tokens and rate per user, with spend alerts. And red team before launch, logging prompts and tool calls for audit with personal data redacted.", "The takeaway: Treat retrieved content as data, least-privilege tools, human approval for irreversible actions, and input and output checks.", "Pause and answer: How should tool and retrieved content be treated?", "⏸", "Answer: As untrusted data, never instructions."]]], "inf": [[null, ["LLM inference is its own engineering discipline, and it comes up constantly in interviews. The key is understanding the two phases of generation."]], ["prefill-vs-decode", ["Generation has two phases with different bottlenecks. Prefill processes the whole prompt in parallel: lots of matrix multiplication, so it's compute bound, and it determines time to first token. Decode produces one token per step: each step must read all the weights and the KV cache from memory for very little computation, so it's memory bandwidth bound, and it determines the time per output token. The KV cache size is two times layers times KV heads times head dimension times sequence length times batch size times bytes. In practice, the number of concurrent requests you can serve is usually limited by KV cache memory, not compute.", "The takeaway: Prefill is compute-bound (TTFT); decode is memory-bandwidth-bound (TPOT); the KV cache grows with sequence and batch.", "Pause and answer: Which phase sets time to first token?", "⏸", "Answer: Prefill."]], ["kv-cache-techniques", ["Techniques for the KV cache. PagedAttention, from vLLM, stores the cache in fixed size blocks with a block table, just like virtual memory in an operating system, which nearly eliminates fragmentation and allows sharing. Prefix caching, or SGLang's RadixAttention, reuses the cache for shared prefixes like a system prompt or common RAG context. Tiered offload moves cold cache from GPU memory to CPU memory, SSD or object storage. Quantising the cache to fp8 or int8 fits more concurrent sequences. And don't confuse the KV cache with a response cache: one stores attention state inside the engine, the other stores whole answers at the application level.", "The takeaway: PagedAttention removes fragmentation, prefix caching reuses shared prompts, quantised and compressed caches save memory.", "Pause and answer: What does PagedAttention borrow from operating systems?", "⏸", "Answer: Virtual-memory-style fixed blocks with a block table."]], ["symptom-lever", ["This table maps symptoms to levers. GPU idle between requests: continuous batching, which adds and removes requests every step. Long prompts slowing everyone: chunked prefill. Prefill bursts starving decode: disaggregated serving with separate prefill and decode pools. The same system prompt reprocessed: prefix caching. Identical full requests: a response cache. Out of memory under concurrency: paged KV and tiered offload. Another replica already has the prefix: KV aware routing. And a model too big for one GPU: tensor or pipeline parallelism.", "The takeaway: Idle GPUs → continuous batching; long prompts → chunked prefill; prefill starving decode → disaggregated serving.", "Pause and answer: GPU idle between requests?", "⏸", "Answer: Continuous batching."]], ["parallelism-quantisation", ["Parallelism and quantisation for serving. Tensor parallelism works within a node over NVLink, pipeline parallelism across nodes, expert parallelism for mixtures of experts, and data parallel replicas for throughput. The precision ladder runs from fp32 to bf16 to fp8 to int8 to int4. Weight only methods like GPTQ and AWQ quantise to four or eight bits; fp8 is native on the newest GPUs. As a rough guide, int8 halves memory with under one percent quality loss, and int4 cuts memory by three quarters with one to three percent loss, but always validate with your evals. Note the difference: QLoRA quantises for training, AWQ and GPTQ for serving. And speculative decoding can speed up decode two to four times when the draft model's tokens are accepted more than seventy percent of the time.", "The takeaway: Tensor parallel within a node, pipeline across nodes, expert parallel for MoE; quantise weights and KV to fit and speed up.", "Pause and answer: Where should tensor parallelism run?", "⏸", "Answer: Within a node over NVLink."]], ["numbers-to-know", ["Numbers to know. One token is about three quarters of a word or four characters. Each billion parameters needs about two gigabytes in bf16, so a seven or eight billion parameter model fits on one twenty four gigabyte GPU, and seventy billion needs about a hundred and forty gigabytes, or two eighty gigabyte GPUs. Aim for a time to first token under a second at the ninety fifth percentile for chat, and twenty to fifty milliseconds per output token, since people read at roughly two hundred milliseconds per token. Guardrail classifiers should take under twenty milliseconds, and IDE completions under a hundred. A semantic cache with a similarity threshold around point nine two can hit forty percent of FAQ traffic. Scale GPUs at sixty to seventy percent utilisation. And consumer peaks can be twelve times the average.", "The takeaway: 1 token ≈ 0.75 words; ~2 GB VRAM per billion params in bf16; 70B needs about 140 GB; TTFT under 1 s p95.", "Pause and answer: VRAM for a 7–8B model in bf16?", "⏸", "Answer: About 14–16 GB."]], ["cost-cascade", ["The cost cascade, in order. First a semantic cache, which answers a share of queries for free. Then a router that sends easy queries to a small model, around twenty times cheaper, and hard ones to a frontier model. Then prompt compression. Then continuous batching and speculative decoding if you self host. Then quantisation and serving many LoRA adapters on one base model. And protect quality with evaluation gates that block cost changes that degrade results.", "The takeaway: Semantic cache, model routing, prompt compression, batching, quantisation and distillation stack up savings.", "Pause and answer: First rung of the cost cascade?", "⏸", "Answer: A semantic cache."]]], "llmsd": [[null, ["Now the system design interview for LLM products and production ML in general."]], ["llm-system-design-framework", ["A five step framework for LLM system design. First scope for five to ten minutes: interactive or batch, queries per second, latency targets for first token and per token, cost per query, and back of the envelope token and GPU estimates. Then a high level design: client, gateway, orchestrator, retriever, model. Then deep dives into two or three components, giving two or three options each and a recommendation. Then wrap up with observability, cost tracking and personal data redaction. And expect curveballs: hallucination, ten times traffic, a vector database running out of memory. The failure mode table gives the standard mitigations, and the rule at the end is the best advice on the card: never name a technology without the reason and a number.", "The takeaway: Scope with estimates, a high-level design, deep dives on retrieval, serving and evaluation, then cost, safety and trade-offs.", "Pause and answer: What belongs in scoping?", "⏸", "Answer: Interactive versus batch, QPS, TTFT/TPOT, cost per query, back-of-envelope tokens and GPUs."]], ["production-ml-mlops", ["Production ML in one table. Deploy through shadow, canary and A/B tests, with blue green switches and rollback. Serve over REST or gRPC with dynamic batching, and optimise with ONNX or TensorRT. Feature stores keep online and offline features consistent with point in time correctness, so training matches serving. Streaming uses Kafka and windowed processing with watermarks. Data platforms use lambda or kappa architectures, open table formats and data contracts. A model registry tracks stages, champions and challengers, and lineage. Monitoring separates data drift from concept drift. And for diagnosis: a sudden drop usually means a pipeline bug, while a gradual decline usually means drift and calls for retraining.", "The takeaway: Shadow, canary and A/B deployment; batched serving; online and offline feature stores with point-in-time correctness; monitoring and retraining.", "Pause and answer: Safe rollout order?", "⏸", "Answer: Shadow → canary → A/B, with rollback."]], ["case-studies-in-your-notes-llm-system-design", ["This card lists the case studies in your LLM system design notes, grouped into RAG and search, agents and MCP, fine tuning and prompting, real time and edge, regulated domains, and LLMOps. They're good practice: pick one, set a forty five minute timer, and design it out loud.", "The takeaway: RAG, search, extraction, assistants and review systems are the classic LLM system design cases.", "Pause and answer: Name two RAG and search case studies.", "⏸", "Answer: Any two of: support chatbot, enterprise semantic search, financial document extraction, shopping assistant, legal contract review."]], ["mlops-lifecycle", ["The MLOps lifecycle diagram. Data and labels are validated for schema and drift, then training and tuning, then an offline evaluation gate. Passing models enter the registry and roll out through shadow and canary to full traffic. Monitoring watches drift, quality, latency and cost; a retrain trigger sends you back to data, and a regression triggers an automatic rollback to the previous model.", "The takeaway: Validate data, train, gate on offline evaluation, register, deploy safely, monitor and retrain.", "Pause and answer: What happens if the offline eval gate fails?", "⏸", "Answer: Back to training."]]], "mlsd": [[null, ["Classic ML system design questions, the non LLM ones, still dominate many ML engineer loops. Here's the framework and ten common prompts."]], ["framework-45-min", ["The forty five minute framework. Clarify the product goal, users, scale and latency budget. Frame it as an ML task, with input, output and label, and ask whether ML is even needed. Choose offline metrics that track the online goal, plus guardrails. Discuss data: sources, labels, sampling, leakage and privacy. Then features: user, item, context and crosses, with freshness and a feature store. Then the model: a baseline first, then something stronger, justified by data size and latency. Then serving: batch or online, retrieval then ranking, caching and fallbacks. Then evaluation with offline replay and A/B tests. And finally monitoring and iteration: drift, feedback loops and retraining cadence.", "The takeaway: Clarify, frame as ML, choose metrics, data and features, model, serving, evaluation and monitoring.", "Pause and answer: What should offline metrics be tied to?", "⏸", "Answer: Online business metrics such as CTR or retention."]], ["common-traps", ["The common traps. Jumping to a deep model before having a baseline and a metric. Training serving skew, where features are computed differently online. Label leakage from signals that only exist after the event, like a refund flag in fraud features. Ignoring position bias in implicit feedback. Random splits on time dependent data. No plan for cold start, delayed labels or imbalance. And forgetting latency and cost, because a hundred millisecond budget shapes the whole design.", "The takeaway: Skipping baselines, training–serving skew, label leakage, ignoring feedback loops and offline-only evaluation.", "Pause and answer: What's training–serving skew?", "⏸", "Answer: Features computed differently online than in training."]], ["ten-prompts-key-decisions", ["These ten prompts cover most interviews. For each, know the label, the key features, the model and serving, and the gotcha. Feed ranking predicts several engagement signals at once and must avoid rewarding engagement bait. Ads click prediction must be calibrated, because the probability feeds into auction bids. Video recommendations optimise watch time and satisfaction, with a freshness boost for new uploads. Search ranking uses graded relevance and corrects for position bias in clicks. Fraud detection deals with delayed chargeback labels, extreme imbalance and adapting adversaries, all within a hundred milliseconds. ETA prediction has asymmetric costs, because being late hurts more than being early. Spam classification needs high precision at high recall, with a human review queue. People you may know uses graph proximity and must respect privacy. Dynamic pricing combines a demand model with an optimiser and raises fairness questions. And visual search uses image and text embeddings with an index that must stay fresh.", "The takeaway: Feed ranking, ads, search, fraud and other prompts each have a label, key features, model and a gotcha.", "Pause and answer: Feed ranking objective?", "⏸", "Answer: Multi-task: click, like, dwell, hide, combined with weights."]]], "mlworked": [[null, ["These are three full model answers that follow the framework. I'll walk you through each like a mock interview."]], ["video-recommendations-home-feed", ["Video recommendations for a home feed. The goal is long term satisfaction, not clicks, so the proxy is expected watch time plus survey based satisfaction, with retention and diversity online and latency and fairness as guardrails. Positive labels are meaningful watches, like thirty seconds or half the video, and negatives are impressions that were skipped. Log the position each video was shown in and train with it as a feature, then fix it to a constant at serving time, which removes position bias. Features include the user's recent watch sequence, the video's content embedding and engagement rates, and cross features like affinity between user and creator. Retrieval uses a two tower model with an approximate nearest neighbour index refreshed hourly; ranking uses a multi task network whose heads are combined with tuned weights. The latency budget is about two hundred milliseconds: thousands of candidates, hundreds ranked, about twenty shown. The trade-offs are exploration for new videos versus short term watch time, cold start, and diversity constraints that stop the feed narrowing a person's tastes.", "The takeaway: Cheap retrieval over millions, an expensive ranker over hundreds, then policy and diversity re-ranking.", "Pause and answer: Why a multi-stage funnel?", "⏸", "Answer: Expensive models can only score hundreds of candidates in time."]], ["payment-fraud-detection", ["Payment fraud detection. This is a synchronous decision under a hundred milliseconds, with heavily imbalanced and delayed labels, and an adversary who adapts. Predict the probability of fraud, then map it to approve, step up authentication, manual review or decline, with thresholds set from the cost of fraud versus the revenue lost to false declines. Measure with PR AUC, recall at a fixed false positive rate, and dollar weighted recall. Chargebacks arrive thirty to ninety days later, so train on matured windows and use analyst labels for recent data. Declined transactions never get labels, so keep a small randomised holdout. Features include velocity counts per card, device, IP and email over several windows, behaviour compared with the user's history, and graph links to known fraud. The model is gradient boosted trees plus hard rules, explained with SHAP for analysts. New models run in shadow mode first, rules are the fallback if the model times out, and you alert on shifts in approval rate and feature drift, because fraudsters adapt within days.", "The takeaway: Synchronous decisions under 100 ms with imbalanced, delayed labels and an adaptive adversary.", "Pause and answer: Latency budget for fraud scoring?", "⏸", "Answer: Under about 100 ms, synchronous."]], ["enterprise-knowledge-assistant-rag", ["An enterprise knowledge assistant. Ten thousand employees, fifty thousand questions a day, first token under two seconds, sources updated within an hour, answers with citations, and never leaking a document someone isn't allowed to open. Measure retrieval recall on a labelled set, faithfulness and correctness with a judge calibrated against humans, citation accuracy and deflection. Ingest by chunking along headings and keeping metadata like source, owner, access groups and update time; change events trigger incremental reindexing and deletes. Retrieve with hybrid search, filtered by the user's permissions inside retrieval, never after generation, then re-rank and keep the best five to eight chunks. The prompt says answer only from the sources, cite them, and say when the answer isn't there. For safety, treat retrieved text as data, because documents can contain prompt injections, and give the model no write tools. Control cost with a small model for query rewriting and a semantic cache scoped by permission set. And gate every change with a golden set of a few hundred questions.", "The takeaway: Grounded, cited answers over internal docs, where permissions and prompt injection matter as much as quality.", "Pause and answer: How are permissions enforced?", "⏸", "Answer: Filter retrieval by the user's access rights."]]], "classic-ai": [[null, ["Before deep learning, AI meant search, games, constraints and probabilistic reasoning. These ideas still power planners, game engines and agent tool use."]], ["search", ["Search algorithms. BFS and DFS are uninformed, and BFS is optimal when every step costs the same. Uniform cost search, which is Dijkstra, is optimal for non negative costs. Iterative deepening gets BFS's optimality with DFS's memory. Greedy best first follows a heuristic and is fast but not optimal. A star combines the cost so far with a heuristic estimate of the cost remaining, and it's optimal if the heuristic never overestimates. And local search, like hill climbing and simulated annealing, improves a single candidate step by step.", "The takeaway: BFS and DFS are uninformed, uniform-cost is optimal for non-negative costs, A* is optimal with an admissible heuristic.", "Pause and answer: When is A* optimal?", "⏸", "Answer: With an admissible (never overestimating) heuristic."]], ["games-and-constraints", ["Games and constraints. Minimax maximises your worst case outcome in two player zero sum games. Alpha beta pruning skips branches that can't change the decision, and with good move ordering it searches about twice as deep in the same time. Monte Carlo tree search repeats selection, expansion, simulation and backpropagation, and AlphaGo combined it with policy and value networks. Constraint satisfaction problems define variables, domains and constraints, solved with backtracking plus heuristics like choosing the most constrained variable first, forward checking and arc consistency.", "The takeaway: Minimax with alpha-beta pruning, MCTS for huge trees, and CSPs with backtracking and propagation.", "Pause and answer: Best-case complexity of alpha-beta?", "⏸", "Answer: O(b^(d/2))."]], ["reasoning-under-uncertainty", ["Reasoning under uncertainty. Bayesian networks encode conditional probabilities on a directed acyclic graph. Hidden Markov models use the forward algorithm for likelihood, Viterbi for the most likely state path, and Baum Welch for learning. Kalman and particle filters track a hidden state from noisy sensors. Markov decision processes are solved with value or policy iteration. And classical logic and planning languages like PDDL still underpin symbolic planners.", "The takeaway: Bayesian networks, HMMs with forward and Viterbi, and Kalman filters handle uncertain state.", "Pause and answer: What does the Viterbi algorithm find?", "⏸", "Answer: The most likely hidden state path."]]], "qbank": [[null, ["These are rapid fire questions from ML and LLM screens. I'll give you the question and the crisp answer, the way you'd say it."]], ["question-crisp-answer", ["Why divide attention scores by the square root of the key dimension? Because dot products grow with dimension, and scaling keeps the softmax out of saturated regions where gradients vanish. L1 versus L2? L1 drives weights exactly to zero, giving feature selection; L2 shrinks smoothly and handles correlated features better. Bagging versus boosting? Bagging cuts variance by averaging independent models; boosting cuts bias by fitting residuals sequentially. Class imbalance? Use the right metric, class weights or focal loss, resample only inside cross validation, and tune the threshold. Why LayerNorm in transformers? It normalises per token, independent of batch size and padding, so it works with a batch of one. What does BatchNorm do at inference? It uses the running statistics from training. Vanishing gradients? ReLU family activations, residual connections, normalisation, good initialisation, and gated RNNs. Why AdamW? It decouples weight decay from the adaptive learning rate. What's the KV cache?"]], ["question-crisp-answer", ["Stored keys and values of past tokens, so each decode step only computes attention for the new token. Why grouped query attention? It shares key value heads, shrinking the cache with little quality loss. How does LoRA work? It freezes the weights and learns a low rank update that can be merged afterwards. DPO versus PPO? DPO learns directly from preference pairs without a reward model, simpler and stabler, while PPO can explore beyond the data. RAG or fine tuning? RAG for changing or private knowledge with citations; fine tuning for consistent behaviour or a cheaper model. Why is speculative decoding lossless? Because the target model verifies draft tokens with rejection sampling, so outputs follow its distribution exactly. Why does temperature zero still vary? Floating point non determinism in batched GPU kernels and ties. How do you evaluate RAG? Retrieval recall and precision, then faithfulness and relevance, then end to end review on a golden set."]], ["question-crisp-answer", ["Data drift versus concept drift? Data drift changes the inputs; concept drift changes the relationship between inputs and labels. Cross validation for time series? Forward chaining splits, never shuffling the future into training. The curse of dimensionality? Distances concentrate and data becomes sparse. ROC AUC for a product manager? The chance the model ranks a random positive above a random negative. Calibration? Predicted probabilities matching observed frequencies, fixed with Platt scaling, isotonic regression or temperature scaling. Why does dropout help? It prevents co-adaptation and approximates an ensemble. And how do you cut LLM serving cost? Cache, route to small models, shorten prompts, quantise, batch continuously, use prefix caching and distil.", "The takeaway: Rapid-fire questions with crisp answers: attention scaling, L1 versus L2, bias–variance and more.", "Pause and answer: Why scale attention by √d_k?", "⏸", "Answer: Dot products grow with dimension; scaling keeps softmax out of saturated regions."]]], "scratch": [[null, ["ML engineer loops often ask you to implement a core algorithm in plain NumPy. These are compact, correct versions. Rather than reading code, I'll tell you the idea and the detail that interviewers look for."]], ["stable-softmax-and-cross-entropy", ["Stable softmax subtracts the maximum of each row before exponentiating. Mathematically this changes nothing, because the shift cancels in the ratio, but it prevents overflow when scores are large. Cross entropy works in log space with the same trick, which is the log sum exp trick. And remember the beautiful gradient: the derivative of cross entropy with respect to the logits is simply the softmax probabilities minus the one hot labels, divided by the batch size.", "The takeaway: Subtract the row max before exponentiating, and use log-softmax for cross-entropy.", "Pause and answer: Why subtract the max before exp?", "⏸", "Answer: To avoid overflow without changing the result."]], ["logistic-regression-with-gradient-descent", ["Logistic regression with gradient descent. Compute predicted probabilities with the sigmoid, then the gradient of the log loss with respect to the weights is X transposed times the errors, divided by n, plus the L2 term. The bias gradient is the mean error. Update both and repeat. Predict positive when the probability is at least a half, or better, at a threshold chosen from costs.", "The takeaway: Sigmoid probabilities, gradient Xᵀ(p − y)/n, optional L2, and a learning rate.", "Pause and answer: Gradient of logistic loss with respect to w?", "⏸", "Answer: Xᵀ(p − y) ÷ n (plus the L2 term)."]], ["k-means", ["K-means alternates two steps. Assign each point to its nearest centroid, using squared distances computed for all pairs at once with broadcasting. Then move each centroid to the mean of its assigned points, keeping the old centroid if a cluster ends up empty. Stop when centroids stop moving. The comment notes that k-means plus plus initialisation, which spreads the initial centroids apart, gives better results than random choice.", "The takeaway: Assign points to the nearest centroid, recompute centroids, repeat; k-means++ initialises better.", "Pause and answer: Two alternating steps of k-means?", "⏸", "Answer: Assign to nearest centroid; recompute centroids as means."]], ["scaled-dot-product-attention-with-a-causal-mask", ["Attention from scratch. Multiply queries by keys transposed and divide by the square root of the dimension. For a causal mask, set every score above the diagonal to a large negative number, so after softmax those future positions get zero weight. Then the softmax weights times the values give the output. For multi head attention, split the dimension into heads, run this per head, concatenate and project.", "The takeaway: Scores = QKᵀ/√d, mask future positions to −∞, softmax, then multiply by V.", "Pause and answer: How is the causal mask applied?", "⏸", "Answer: Set scores above the diagonal to −∞ before softmax."]], ["roc-auc-from-scratch", ["ROC AUC from scratch uses its probabilistic meaning. Rank all scores, averaging ranks for ties. The sum of the positives' ranks, minus the smallest possible sum, divided by the number of positive negative pairs, is the fraction of pairs where the positive outranks the negative. That's the Mann Whitney U statistic in disguise, and it avoids building the curve at all.", "The takeaway: AUC is the probability a random positive outranks a random negative, computed from ranks.", "Pause and answer: What does ROC-AUC mean probabilistically?", "⏸", "Answer: P(random positive scores above random negative), ties counting half."]], ["top-p-nucleus-sampling", ["Top p sampling. Apply temperature and softmax, sort tokens by probability, take the cumulative sum, keep the smallest prefix whose mass reaches p, renormalise those probabilities, and sample. It adapts to the model's confidence: when one token dominates, the set is tiny; when many are plausible, it's larger.", "The takeaway: Sort probabilities, keep the smallest set reaching p, renormalise and sample.", "Pause and answer: Which tokens survive top-p?", "⏸", "Answer: The smallest set whose cumulative probability reaches p."]]], "last": [[null, ["We've reached the last minute checklist. Here it is, as I'd tell you right before you walk in."]], ["last-minute-revision", ["Baseline first, gradient boosting for tabular data, and split before you fit anything. For imbalance, use PR AUC or F1, class weights, and resample only inside cross validation. Attention is softmax of Q K transposed over root d, times V; grouped query attention shrinks the KV cache; RoPE encodes positions; and modern blocks use pre norm, RMSNorm and SwiGLU. Train with AdamW, warmup and cosine decay, clip gradients at one, and use bf16. Supervised fine tuning for format, DPO for preferences, LoRA and QLoRA for cheap adaptation. RAG means hybrid retrieval, re-ranking and faithfulness evaluation, with chunks of a few hundred tokens. Prefill is compute bound and sets time to first token; decode is memory bound and sets time per token; and the KV cache limits concurrency. vLLM gives you paged attention, continuous batching, prefix caching, disaggregation and speculative decoding. And quantise with fp8 on the newest hardware or int4 with AWQ or GPTQ to fit, always validating with evals.", "The takeaway: Baselines first, GBDT for tabular, split before fitting, PR-AUC for imbalance, attention and serving essentials.", "Pause and answer: Metric for imbalanced classification?", "⏸", "Answer: PR-AUC or F1."]], [null, ["That's the whole ML, AI and LLM sheet. It's a lot, so don't try to absorb it in one sitting. Listen to one area at a time, then test yourself with the flashcards."]]]};
